#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a1b6111-9dc6-503f-b15f-e1d5b9318879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-2,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281652} {:answer/id #uuid "c7657607-7e70-5bae-b66e-881b33afc8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [5,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653} {:answer/id #uuid "64387a76-ce7e-5bd4-acb5-b222270b468d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [-2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281654} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b43c59-0b32-5d38-8ef9-f3c9bd2da55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281655} {:answer/id #uuid "c561cad0-2cab-5d53-8498-ade66a4f8f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6b43c59-0b32-5d38-8ef9-f3c9bd2da55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281655} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c77fba6-32d1-5c60-b147-17c6b7879e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "045cd359-00fc-5634-ad0d-5baf72815ca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-27073" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $5{x}^{2}+25x-53\\ge 10x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+25x-53 & \\ge 10x-3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+15x-50 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+3x-10) & \\ge 0 \\\\ 5(x+5)(x-2) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $5(x+5)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $5(x+5)(x-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $5(x+5)(x-2)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $5(x+5)(x-2)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $5{x}^{2}+25x-53\\ge 10x-3$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00bf9646-ccdb-5188-a4c3-44ae725df660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281659} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f2a3b6-f2a5-5f00-b0f2-ffde3b122e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00bf9646-ccdb-5188-a4c3-44ae725df660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281659} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1445b42-2d2f-5280-8c58-38f0d91402cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef6185a4-154b-597d-b8ca-7f280bb5fa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef6185a4-154b-597d-b8ca-7f280bb5fa39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d091ff-01c5-5d0e-8b5a-5dbf7a0263cb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b0f5feb-954b-5655-a9ed-e61dd455cc7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277165" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+16x+20<3x-22?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+16x+20 & <3x-22 \\\\ {x}^{2}+13x+42 & <0 \\\\ (x+7)(x+6) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x+6)=0.$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=-6,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,-6),$ and $(-6,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,-6)$ | $(-6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x+6)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x+6)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x+6)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-7,-6).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+16x+20<3x-22$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-7,-6).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d719cdae-8d37-585f-a9b6-4542a046d1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d719cdae-8d37-585f-a9b6-4542a046d1bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281665} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6d82c6-5432-598d-b341-6811e404ae0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be226a0-d248-521a-b64e-06abf1ef4132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8be226a0-d248-521a-b64e-06abf1ef4132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281667} :answer-field/id #uuid "77ba59f2-bbf5-52d3-a01c-2ea1b3a2422c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281666} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f327c3-23d8-539c-be0c-c1d721722e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f327c3-23d8-539c-be0c-c1d721722e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281669} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fd521a5-4966-564a-bcf1-ebcfaed92b30" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01a5dd0a-1735-525a-9678-ebead93afb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-277167" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-2x-8\\ge 0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]\\cup [$\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-4) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-4)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-4)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-2$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-2x-8\\ge 0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [4,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0362876a-eb34-5489-8765-d399a5006fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0362876a-eb34-5489-8765-d399a5006fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281672} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1dbb65-434c-5d86-ba03-f24a94e7363e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281671} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa748593-1c18-5ae5-b68a-9c9d25abbfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa748593-1c18-5ae5-b68a-9c9d25abbfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281674} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0189036-fa12-50b4-adec-84e8c8d0b104" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9fb8a16-ea39-5282-83e7-7446a57d46fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-278266" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+5x<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x & <0 \\\\ x(x+5) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+5)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+5)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-5,0).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+5x<0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-5,0).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0f738c1-8113-598b-8b2f-7634fc587c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281677} {:answer/id #uuid "662c4882-089e-59d6-8192-46224846ca48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281678} {:answer/id #uuid "82fbec2e-745f-5d98-a23a-0850c70c33b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281679} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d05a70-fc82-51ef-96fc-03fdd390b833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281680} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9fb0d0-c29f-520c-88b7-f50f3ea7867e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662c4882-089e-59d6-8192-46224846ca48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-3,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281678} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6383ca9-a190-5afe-bcda-bff7851e9f97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "97935460-3f45-5907-8a6e-4ba8b0a30ece" :question/math-academy-id "q-70919" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-6 & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+3)(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+3)(x-2)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+3)(x-2)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+x-6\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-3,2].$"}]}
